+# AutoGuard 26.3 verification notes
+
+## Inspected directly
+
+- Fabric API `26.3` source on GitHub ([`FabricMC/fabric`, branch `26.3`](https://github.com/FabricMC/fabric/tree/26.3)): `KeyMappingHelper`, key-mapping API, `ClientCommandRegistrationCallback` / `ClientCommands`, `AttackBlockCallback`, `AttackEntityCallback`, and the HUD extraction API (`HudElementRegistry`, `HudElement`, `GuiGraphicsExtractor`). This is why the project uses the 26.3 SDL-era key APIs and GUI extraction methods rather than older GLFW / `HudRenderCallback` / `GuiGraphics` examples.
+- A public Fabric 26.3 project as a toolchain example ([`LZaruba/morning-stretch`](https://github.com/LZaruba/morning-stretch)): `net.fabricmc.fabric-loom` 1.19.3, Gradle 9.8.2, Java 25, no mappings, and `implementation` dependencies for Fabric Loader/API.
+
+## Not verified
+
+- No Mojang 26.3 client/server jar or decompiled Minecraft source was available in this environment. Therefore this project has **not** verified the vanilla implementation of shield block angle, raise delay, which damage types/projectiles are blocked, shield durability loss, knockback, explosion blocking, shield-disable cooldown, or exact hand-use/release behavior.
+- Whether neutral-mob anger target state is synchronized to a remote client was not verified. The detector avoids `Mob.getTarget()` and instead uses client-visible position, velocity, facing, and swing cues, except that angry-neutral classification still relies on the client-visible `NeutralMob.isAngryAt(player)` method.
+- The mod was not compiled, launched, or tested with the requested other mods. Java is absent in the workspace; Gradle could not start. The GitHub Actions workflow is the intended build check once pushed to a GitHub repository.
+
+## Conservative implementation choices / assumptions
+
+- Creeper reaction defaults to **off** because shield mitigation of a creeper blast was not verified. The option exists but must not be enabled for reliance until tested in vanilla 26.3.
+- The current threat detector treats hostile direct melee and approaching projectiles as candidates, with a configurable player-facing arc and early range margin. It is heuristic: it is not a proof that every detected hit's damage type is blockable.
+- Shield raise and release use `MultiPlayerGameMode.useItem(player, hand)` and `releaseUsingItem(player)` through the explicit `InteractionHand`; this API choice is based on the ordinary Fabric client interaction pattern, but exact vanilla 26.3 packet/hand semantics still require source or runtime confirmation.
+- There is no compile or in-game pass claim. Resolve any 26.3 symbol/API mismatches and then run the README checklist before distributing a jar.
